Consider the following solution to the producer-consumer synchronization problem. The shared buffer size is $N$. Three semaphores $empty$, $full$ and $\text{mutex}$ are defined with respective initial values of $0$, $N$ and $1$. Semaphore $empty$ denotes the number of available slots in the buffer, for the consumer to read from. Semaphore $full$ denotes the number of available slots in the buffer, for the producer to write to. The placeholder variables, denoted by $\text{P}$, $\text{Q}$, $\text{R}$, and $\text{S}$, in the code below can be assigned either $empty$ or $full$. The valid semaphore operations are: $\text{wait}()$ and $\text{signal}()$.<br><br><div style="overflow-x: auto; margin: 12px 0;"><table style="border-collapse: collapse; border: 1px solid #ffffff; width: 100%; font-size: 13px;"><thead><tr><th style="border: 1px solid #ffffff; padding: 8px 12px; text-align: left; font-weight: bold; width: 50%;">Producer:</th><th style="border: 1px solid #ffffff; padding: 8px 12px; text-align: left; font-weight: bold; width: 50%;">Consumer:</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="border: 1px solid #ffffff; padding: 12px; font-family: monospace; white-space: pre; vertical-align: top;">do {
    wait(P);
    wait(mutex);
    //Add item to buffer
    signal(mutex);
    signal(Q);
} while(1);</td><td style="border: 1px solid #ffffff; padding: 12px; font-family: monospace; white-space: pre; vertical-align: top;">do {
    wait(R);
    wait(mutex);
    //Consume item from buffer
    signal(mutex);
    signal(S);
} while(1);</td></tr></tbody></table></div><br>Which one of the following assignments to $\text{P}$, $\text{Q}$, $\text{R}$ and $\text{S}$ will yield the correct solution?
